    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure is coherent, additive, and idiomatic for this codebase:\n\n- **`toRiskFlag` sits in the right unit.** `OwnerMapper` already derives read-only DTO fields via sibling `default` helpers (`toAgeBand`, `toFiscalYear`, `toOwnerSegment`), each wired with an `expression = \"java(...)\"` mapping. `riskFlag` follows that exact established pattern, so a maintainer would find it exactly where they'd expect the other derived fields.\n\n- **The extraction removes duplication rather than adding it.** The disposable-domain blocklist and the domain-parsing logic previously lived privately inside `EnsureEmailDomainAllowed`. Pulling them into a single `EmailDomains` model class means the create-time rejection and the read-time risk signal now share one authoritative definition, instead of each re-implementing `domainOf`/blocklist. That's the right direction, and `EnsureEmailDomainAllowed` is correctly slimmed to delegate.\n\n- **`EmailDomains` is well-scoped, not a catch-all.** It owns exactly one concept (what \"disposable\" and \"disposable-adjacent\" mean), with a private constructor, a focused public surface (`DISPOSABLE`, `domainOf`, `isDisposableAdjacent`), and a private `DISPOSABLE_LABELS` detail. The placement note (beside `Owner`, to avoid a package cycle) is a sound, deliberate choice.\n\n- **Composition reads cleanly.** `toRiskFlag` names each of the three spec signals as a local boolean before OR-ing them, mirroring the spec sentence, and the Javadoc plus the OpenAPI description keep the derived-on-read semantics documented at both the code and contract edges.\n\nNo duplicated logic, no unit is becoming a grab-bag, and the split between \"reject outright\" (function pipeline) vs. \"surface as soft signal\" (mapper) matches the codebase's separation of request handling from DTO derivation.",
